body:
@marco-ippolito, I like your idea to make `Cookie.from()` return an Iterable. This can simplify working with multiple cookies. But at the same time, if we tackle the simplest scenario of the cookie string representing a single cookie, returning an Iterable and having to iterate over it to grab a single value looks like a design flaw. I'd like to think on this a bit more. We should have a way to cover the following scenarios:

- A cookie string with 1 cookie -> a `Cookie` instance;
- A cookie string with N cookies -> an `Iterator<Cookie>` for each cookie described in the string. 

Not sure what I think about making the `Cookie.from()` method too smart to handle both of those use cases for the developer. 

> Also what's the behavior of isExpired if expired is not set explicitly?

I believe newly created cookies that do not have the `expired` property set explicitly must not be marked as expired. I'd like them to have a sensible default here. I'm sure there must be something in the spec regarding the cookie expiration. If I send a cookie without an explicit expiration date from a server, what date will the browser treat as its expiration date? 

> Regarding secure, httpOnly and partitioned, if they are not set at creation time, doesn't that mean they get implicitly set as false? Does the undefined value make sense?

@mkarajohn, a great question. I think it's an oversight on my part. `secure`, `httpOnly`, and `partitioned` properties must be booleans, never `undefined`. Regarding their default values, I propose the following:

- `partitioned` is `false` unless explicitly provided;
- `httpOnly` is `true` unless explicitly provided (sensible default for security considerations).
- `secure` is `true` unless explicitly provided (again, sensible security default).

If this behavior makes sense, I can add it to the proposal above so it's clear for everyone.

I think `from` and `serialize` should use spread notation as argoument.
On second thought returning a `Map` feels the best option.
```javascript
Cookie.serialize(...[
  new Cookie({ name: 'secret', value: 'abc-123' }),
  new Cookie({ name: 'foo', value: 'bar' })
])

Cookie.serialize(new Cookie({ name: 'foo', value: 'bar' }))

// real life case
const response = await fetch('foo');
const cookies = Cookie.from(...response.headers.getSetCookie())
const foo = cookies.get('foo').value;

```

@mcollina also proposed using a plain Object as a return value of the `Cookie.parse()` method. That would look like this:

```js
const cookies = Cookie.parse('a=b; c=d')

// Get a cookie by name.
cookies.a // Cookie {}

// Get the list of all cookies.
Object.values(cookies)

// Serialize all the cookies.
Cookie.serialize(...Object.values(cookies))
```

@Mahmoudgalalz, 

> I think also adding the ability to delete a cookie based on its key is needed

You can achieve this using both `Map` and a plain object.

```js
// Using Map:
cookies.delete(cookieName)

// Using plain object:
delete cookies[cookieName]
```

> also, how edit the value within the key does it delete then create, or modify the existing cookie with the same values

If you modify any property on the `Cookie` instance, that modification will be reflected on that cookie once you serialize it to a string.

```js
// Simple example
const cookie = new Cookie('session', 'abc-123')
cookie.name = 'newSession'
cookie.toString() // "newSession=abc-123"

// Example with "Cookie.parse()"
const cookies = Cookie.parse(cookieString)
const sessionCookie = cookies.session
sessionCookie.name = 'newSession'
Cookie.serialize(...Object.values(cookies))
// "newSession=abc-123"
```
